      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written, even with your confirmation. I know of no indication for ibuprofen at 6,400 mg/day, or 1,600 mg in a single dose. That's twice the maximum labeled daily dose. Even the high-dose regimens used for cystic fibrosis or rheumatologic disease stay well under it. Above roughly 3,200 mg/day, the GI bleeding, kidney injury, and cardiovascular risks go up without any added benefit. I can't verify the clinical rationale from here, and an error would be hard to undo once the patient started taking it.\n\nHere are some options:\n- **Enter the maximum standard dose.** I can enter 800 mg PO four times daily (3,200 mg/day) now, and you can adjust it after review.\n- **Use a pharmacy or attending review.** If there is a documented protocol or specialist recommendation for this dose, pharmacy can verify it and enter it through the proper override pathway. I can add a note to the order asking for that review.\n- **Reconsider the drug.** If the goal is stronger anti-inflammatory or analgesic effect, a different agent or a multimodal regimen may be a better option than going past the ibuprofen ceiling.\n\nTell me which you'd like. If you want the 800 mg four times daily order, I'll enter it right away."
      }
